      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 for water meters types MS2500 / MV110.\n\nThe fragment includes:\n1. Certificate number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\n2. Marking requirements text\n3. Characteristics table with technical data for water meters types MS2500 / MV110\n4. Manufacturer: Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\n5. Model number: MS2500 / MV110\n\nThe table structure:\n- Nominal diameter: 25, 32, 40 (3 nominal diameters)\n- Each nominal diameter has TWO columns (6 columns total):\n  - DN25: columns 1-2\n  - DN32: columns 3-4\n  - DN40: columns 5-6\n\nLet me map out the values:\n\n| | DN25 col1 | DN25 col2 | DN32 col1 | DN32 col2 | DN40 col1 | DN40 col2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Q1 [m³/h]: | 0.064 | 0.040 | 0.100 | 0.062 | 0.160 | 0.100 |\n| Q2 [m³/h]: | 0.102 | 0.064 | 0.160 | 0.100 | 0.256 | 0.160 |\n| Q3 [m³/h]: | 16.00 | 16.00 | 25.00 | 25.00 | 40.00 | 40.00 |\n| Q4 [m³/h]: | 20.00 | 20.00 | 31.25 | 31.25 | 50.00 | 50.00 |\n| Q3/Q1: | 250 | 400 | 250 | 400 | 250 | 400 |\n| Q2/Q1: | 1.6 (all) |\n| Q4/Q3: | 1.25 (all) |\n| Measuring principle: | Electromagnetic water meter (all) |\n| Accuracy class: | 1 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 2 |\n| MPEl: | ±3 % (Class 1), ±5 % (Class 2) |\n| MPEu: | ±1 (class 1) ±2 (class 2) % for water ≤ 30 °C; ±2 (class1) ±3 (class 2) % for water > 30 °C |\n| Temperature class: | For liner PTFE - T90; T50; T30; For liner Rilsan - T50; T30 from DN50; For liner Ebonite - T50; T30 from DN200; For liner polypropylene - T50; T30 |\n| Water pressure class: | MAP16 |\n| Pressure loss class: | Δp40 (all) |\n| Reverse flow: | designed to measure |\n| Environmental class: | O (-25 to +55 °C) |\n| Electromagnetic environment: | E1, E2 |\n| Mechanical class: | B, O |\n| Maximum admissible temperature [°C]: | For liners PTFE - 90°C; For liner Polypropylen, Rilsan and Ebonite - 50°C |\n| Maximum admissible pressure [MPa]: | 1.6 |\n| Orientation limitation: | any |\n| Indicating range [m³]: | 99 999 (all) |\n| Resolution of the indicating device [m³]: | 0.000 1 (all) |\n\nSo there are 6 model variants:\n1. DN25, Q3=16, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\n2. DN25, Q3=16, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\n3. DN32, Q3=25, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\n4. DN32, Q3=25, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\n5. DN40, Q3=40, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\n6. DN40, Q3=40, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\n\nModel IDs could be like \"MS2500/MV110-DN25-Q3=16-Q3/Q1=250\" but let's keep it simpler. Following the R137 example, model_id could be the nominal diameter + metrological class. Let me use something like:\n- \"DN25_Q3=16_R250\"\n- \"DN25_Q3=16_R400\"\n- etc.\n\nActually let me think about the Q3/Q1 designation. In R49 water meters, the \"metrological class\" is often expressed as R (= Q3/Q1). Let me use:\n- \"DN25-R250\" (class 1)\n- \"DN25-R400\" (class 2)\n- \"DN32-R250\" (class 1)\n- \"DN32-R400\" (class 2)\n- \"DN40-R250\" (class 1)\n- \"DN40-R400\" (class 2)\n\nNow the second page (page 5) has additional data:\n\n- Resolution of the device for rapid testing [m³]: \"-\" (no value)\n- EUT testing requirements (OIML R 49-2:2013, 8.1.8):\n  - Category: Electromagnetic water meter\n  - Case: Case D\n- Installation details:\n  - Connection type (screw thread): Flanged\n  - Minimum straight length of inlet pipe [mm]: 0\n  - Minimum straight length of outlet pipe [mm]: 0\n  - Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n  - Flow conditioner (details if required): No\n  - Mounting: -\n  - Orientation: any\n  - Other relevant information: -\n  - Length [mm]: 200, 200, 200 — note: this has 3 columns (one per nominal diameter: DN25, DN32, DN40)! Wait, the row has 3 values: 200, 200, 200. So it's per nominal diameter.\n- Installation details (electrical):\n  - Wiring instructions: -\n  - Mounting arrangement: -\n  - Orientation limitations: -\n- Power supply:\n  - Designation of the module for all nominal diameter: three modules:\n    - MS2500 / MV110 HV\n    - MS2500 / MV110 LV\n    - MS2500 / MV110 LLV\n  - Type (battery, mains AC, mains DC):\n    - HV: 100 – 240 V AC\n    - LV: 24 – 36V AC/DC\n    - LLV: 10 – 48 V DC\n  - Frequency:\n    - HV: 45 – 66 Hz\n    - LV: 45 – 66 Hz\n    - LLV: -\n  - Internal backup source:\n    - HV: 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\n    - LV: 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\n    - LLV: -\n  - Minimum battery life time [years]: -, -, - (no values)\n  - Software version (of legally relevant SW): V.1.04.0005.0004 (all)\n  - CRC checksum (of legally relevant SW): 9BA34F3B (all)\n  - Cut-off: 0.2 %\n  - Detection empty pipe: on\n\nSo this is a 3-D structure: 6 metrological variants × 3 power supply modules. The power supply module is a configuration axis (not per model). So I should use config_level for power supply attributes, with axis \"power_supply_module\" and conditions \"HV\", \"LV\", \"LLV\".\n\nThe \"Length [mm]\" row has 3 values per nominal diameter: config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter\"? Or model_level? The length corresponds to DN: DN25→200, DN32→200, DN40→200. Since the models are defined by DN + R, the length is constant across R, so it could be type_level (all 200) or config_level. Actually since all values are the same (200), I could make it a type_level characteristic: minimum... wait, \"Length\" is the physical length of the meter. value: 200, unit_symbol: \"mm\".\n\nLet me now structure the JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [...],  // not present explicitly in this fragment? Actually Manufacturer: Hemina SPA... but the applicant may have been mentioned on page 1 (not in this fragment). The table says \"Manufacturer: Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\". So I can include manufacturers[]: Hemina SPA.\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\", \"MV110\"],\n    \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meters types MS2500 / MV110\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"MS2500 / MV110\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN25-R250\"},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nWait — should category be \"Water meter\"? The schema examples say \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... — so \"Water meter\" fits the pattern.\n\nThe certificate number is R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02. Scheme A (from \"-A-\" in the number). Recommendation: R49, edition 2013? Actually the format R49/2013 suggests the edition year 2013. The text mentions \"OIML R 49-2:2013\". Hmm, the recommendation R49 has edition 2013. Let me set:\n\n```json\n\"recommendation\": {\n  \"id\": \"R49\",\n  \"edition\": 2013\n}\n```\n\nActually the \"2013\" in the certificate number could be the edition. The requirements reference \"OIML R 49 and OIML R 49-2:2013\". I'll include edition 2013 with a note... no, no notes. I'll set edition: 2013. Hmm, but should I be careful? The rule says don't fabricate. The certificate number contains /2013/ which strongly suggests edition. Also the referenced R 49-2:2013. I think edition 2013 is safe. Accuracy classes: 1 and 2 are mentioned (\"Accuracy class (Class xx)\", and per-model accuracy class 1 or 2). R49 accuracy classes are 1 and 2. I'll include accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nIssuing authority: Český metrologický institut (from the stamp). But that's only partly visible in the stamp. The person info isn't in this fragment. Should I include it? The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut\". I could include issuing_authority.name = \"Český metrologický institut\". Hmm, it's from a stamp annotation. I think it's acceptable but maybe better to omit since the fragment is a fragment and part 1 probably has it. The instruction says \"Return a JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" The stamp is content in this fragment. I'll include the name only, no address. Actually, let me include it — it's present. But partial... I'll include name \"Český metrologický institut\".\n\nHmm, actually, being cautious: the stamp marker is an OCR annotation of an image, not text. But it does identify the issuer. I'll include it with oiml_issuer_id maybe \"CZ1\"? The cert number has \"CZ1\" in it which is the issuer code. Hmm, oiml_issuer_id — not certain. I could set oiml_issuer_id: \"CZ1\"? That's derived from the certificate number pattern \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" where CZ1 is the issuing authority identifier. I think it's reasonable but might be fabrication. I'll include oiml_issuer_id: \"CZ1\" at certificate level? Hmm. The scheme field: \"A\" appears in the number. Let me include certificate.scheme = \"A\".\n\nFor project_number, page_total: \"Page 4 of 16 pages\" → page_total: 16. Good, that's present.\n\nNow the model family. Let me carefully assign model_ids. The 6 variants:\n\nDN25: Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102, Q3=16.00, Q4=20.00, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\nDN25: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=16.00, Q4=20.00, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\nDN32: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=25.00, Q4=31.25, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\nDN32: Q1=0.062, Q2=0.100, Q3=25.00, Q4=31.25, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\nDN40: Q1=0.160, Q2=0.256, Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\nDN40: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00, Q3/Q1=400, class 2\n\nWait, let me check the column ordering. The Q1 row: 0.064, 0.040, 0.100, 0.062, 0.160, 0.100. The Q3/Q1 row: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400. So col1 is R250, col2 is R400. For R250: Q1 = Q3/R = 16/250 = 0.064 ✓. For R400: Q1 = 16/400 = 0.040 ✓. Great, consistent.\n\nQ2 = Q1 × 1.6: 0.064×1.6 = 0.1024 ≈ 0.102 ✓; 0.040×1.6 = 0.064 ✓. Consistent.\n\nModel IDs: I'll use \"DN25-R250\" etc. Or maybe \"DN25_Q3=16_R250\". Simpler: \"DN25-R250\", \"DN25-R400\", \"DN32-R250\", \"DN32-R400\", \"DN40-R250\", \"DN40-R400\".\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- nominal_diameter: DN25→25, DN32→32, DN40→40, unit_symbol: \"mm\"? Nominal diameter DN is usually expressed in mm. The table doesn't give a unit explicitly. Hmm. DN25 means DN 25 mm typically. But to avoid fabrication, I could use value 25 with unit_symbol \"DN\"? Or null. I'll use unit_symbol: \"mm\" — standard for DN. Actually, DN numbers nominally correspond to mm. I think mm is safe. Alternatively unit null. Let me keep \"mm\".\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: to be safe, unit_symbol: null and value 25? That loses info. DN is dimensionless designation but corresponds to mm. I'll go with \"mm\".\n\nActually, hold on. Let me be careful about fabrication. DN 25 = 25 mm nominal. It's a well-known convention. I'll use \"mm\".\n\n- q1 (minimum flowrate): per model values, unit m³/h\n- q2 (transitional flowrate): m³/h\n- q3 (permanent flowrate): m³/h\n- q4 (overload flowrate): m³/h\n- ratio_q3_q1: dimensionless\n- accuracy_class: per model: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2 — these are class designations; value as string \"1\" or number 1? Accuracy class 1/2 — number is fine.\n\ntype_level characteristics (constant across models):\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- q4_q3_ratio: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- mpe_lower_zone... this is per accuracy class really. MPEl: ±3% (class 1), ±5% (class 2). I could put in config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\": class 1 → 3%, class 2 → 5%. Or type_level as strings. Let me use config_level:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute: \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2\", value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]\n\nWait, but the structured value form for config_level: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}` per rule 5. The values in model_level have {model, value, footnote_markers}. For config_level, {condition, value} — plus unit_symbol at the entry level presumably, similar to model_level. Let me mirror model_level structure: attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value, footnote_markers}]. And unit_symbol at attribute level.\n\n2. attribute: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class\" and \"water_temperature\". Two axes. MPEu: ±1% (class 1), ±2% (class 2) for T ≤ 30°C; ±2% (class 1), ±3% (class 2) for T > 30°C. I could use two entries:\n   - axis \"accuracy_class, water_temperature\" with conditions \"class 1, ≤ 30 °C\" → 1, \"class 2, ≤ 30 °C\" → 2, \"class 1, > 30 °C\" → 2, \"class 2, > 30 °C\" → 3. \n   \n   That's a reasonable flattening. axis: \"accuracy_class × water_temperature\".\n\n3. Temperature class: \"For liner PTFE - T90; T50; T30; For liner Rilsan - T50; T30 from DN50; For liner Ebonite - T50; T30 from DN200; For liner polypropylene - T50; T30\". This is a string-valued characteristic, could be config_level with axis \"liner_material\": \n   - PTFE: \"T90, T50, T30\"\n   - Rilsan: \"T50, T30 (from DN50)\"\n   - Ebonite: \"T50, T30 (from DN200)\"\n   - Polypropylene: \"T50, T30\"\n\n4. Maximum admissible temperature: PTFE → 90°C, Polypropylen/Rilsan/Ebonite → 50°C. config_level axis \"liner_material\":\n   - PTFE: 90 °C\n   - Polypropylen: 50, Rilsan: 50, Ebonite: 50. Or one condition \"Polypropylen, Rilsan, Ebonite\" → 50.\n\nLet me do 4 conditions: \"PTFE\"→90; \"Polypropylen\"→50; \"Rilsan\"→50; \"Ebonite\"→50.\n\n5. Maximum admissible pressure: 1.6 MPa — type_level.\n\nOther type_level:\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\" → value \"Δp40\"? Hmm, canonical labels shouldn't include LaTeX. \"Δp40\" is a value string (pressure loss class designation). It's fine as a string value: \"Δp40\". It's a class designation, not a numeric. Keep as string \"Δp40\".\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\" with range -25 to +55 °C. The table says: Environmental class: O (-25 to + 55 °C). I can record environmental_class: \"O\" and environmental_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\".\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\" → string \"E1, E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\" → string \"B, O\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: 99 999 → 99999 m³\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.000 1 → 0.0001 m³\n- power supply stuff is config_level by module.\n- software_version: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"\n- crc_checksum: \"9BA34F3B\"\n- cut_off: 0.2 %\n- detection_empty_pipe: \"on\"\n- resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing: \"-\" → omit (rule 8: empty strings are not values).\n- minimum_battery_life_time: \"-\" → omit.\n- eut_testing_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_testing_case: \"Case D\"\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- mounting: \"-\" → omit\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- length: 200 mm per DN — all same. type_level: 200 mm. But it's per nominal diameter with 3 columns each 200. Since all are 200, type_level works.\n\nPower supply config_level (axis: \"power_supply_module\", conditions \"HV\", \"LV\", \"LLV\"):\n\n1. power_supply_module_designation: \n   - HV: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\"\n   - LV: \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\"\n   - LLV: \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\"\n   \n   Actually this defines the axis conditions themselves. Could be captured as the components? Rule 6: components for sub-assemblies with alternatives. The three modules HV/LV/LLV are alternatives (a meter is built with one of them) — alternatives \"OR\". Hmm, components[] with role \"power supply module\", type_designations [\"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\"], alternatives \"OR\". That could work nicely. But also the config_level axis captures the power characteristics per module.\n\nLet me do both: components[] entry for power supply module with alternatives OR, and config_level for the electrical attributes.\n\n2. power_supply_type: HV: \"AC 100–240 V\"; LV: \"AC/DC 24–36 V\"; LLV: \"DC 10–48 V\".\n   Per rule 3, split voltage and type:\n   - power_supply_voltage: HV {min:100, max:240} V; LV {min:24, max:36} V; LLV {min:10, max:48} V\n   - power_supply_type: HV \"AC\"; LV \"AC/DC\"; LLV \"DC\"\n3. power_supply_frequency: HV {min:45, max:66} Hz; LV {min:45, max:66} Hz; LLV: \"-\" → omit or null. For config values, I can omit the LLV condition or set value null. Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll include only HV and LV conditions.\n4. internal_backup_source: HV \"3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\" — note \"3,7V\" European decimal → 3.7. LV same. LLV: \"-\".\n   I could split: internal_backup_source_voltage: 3.7 V DC; battery_capacity: 5200 mAh; backup_type: Battery. That's cleaner. Or keep as string. Let me split into:\n   - internal_backup_source_voltage: value 3.7, unit \"V\" (DC type)\n   - backup_battery_capacity: value 5200, unit \"mAh\"\n   Or keep it as one string value \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh; Battery\". Hmm. The rules push toward structured separation. I'll split into internal_backup_source_voltage (3.7 V) and internal_backup_battery_capacity (5200 mAh). Maybe also internal_backup_type \"Battery\".\n\nNow, the footnote markers — none in this fragment. Footnotes: none.\n\nMatrix_tables: Should I create a matrix table for the flow characteristics? The table is 2-D: 6 variants × attributes. Following the R137 example, I should emit matrix_tables AND model_family + model_level. Let me create one matrix table \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter and metrological class\" with columns: nominal_dn, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class. And a second table for installation/power? The power supply table is 3-column by module: columns: module, power_supply_type, voltage, frequency, internal_backup_source. Could add matrix_tables for that too. Let me include two matrix tables:\n\n1. \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter and metrological class\" \n   columns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"accuracy_class\"]\n   column_units: [\"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null]\n   rows: 6 rows.\n\n2. \"Power supply modules\"\n   columns: [\"module\", \"power_supply_type\", \"voltage\", \"frequency\", \"internal_backup_source\"]\n   column_units: [null, null, \"V\", \"Hz\", null]\n   rows: 3 rows (HV, LV, LLV). LLV frequency \"-\" → null.\n\nHmm, and maybe a third for installation details per DN? Only length varies per DN and it's all 200. Not needed.\n\nAlso the model_level values — with \"model\" keys referencing the model_ids.\n\nLet me also double check: \"Accuracy class (Class xx)\" appears in the markings list — that's about markings. Not a data value.\n\nDate issued: not in fragment. project_number: not in fragment. member_state: not in fragment (CZ1 suggests Czech but not stated explicitly... the stamp says Český metrologický institut, which is Czech). member_state could be \"Czech Republic\"? Not explicitly stated. I'll omit member_state.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"CZ1\" — I'll include at certificate level? The pattern in cert numbers includes issuer ID. I'll include \"CZ1\". Hmm, risk of fabrication... The number R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 — CZ1 is indeed the CMI's OIML issuer identifier. I'll include it in certificate.oiml_issuer_id. Actually let me be conservative: include it. It's part of the certificate number present in the fragment.\n\nNow characteristic labels (snake_case, no LaTeX):\n- q1 → \"q1\"? Or \"minimum_flowrate_q1\". Canonical: the table uses Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4. I'll use \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\". Hmm, or simpler \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\". The R137 example uses \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin\". So \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\" fit that style. I'll use q1, q2, q3, q4.\n- \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"ratio_q4_q3\".\n- \"nominal_diameter\".\n- \"accuracy_class\".\n- \"measuring_principle\".\n- \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\" — the table label: \"Maximum permissible error for the lower flowrate zone (MPEl)\". Label: \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\".\n- \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\".\n- \"temperature_class\".\n- \"water_pressure_class\": \"MAP16\".\n- \"pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp40\".\n- \"reverse_flow\": \"designed to measure\".\n- \"environmental_class\": \"O\".\n- \"environmental_class_temperature_range\": {min: -25, max: 55} °C.\n- \"electromagnetic_environment\": \"E1, E2\".\n- \"mechanical_class\": \"B, O\".\n- \"maximum_admissible_temperature\".\n- \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": 1.6 MPa.\n- \"orientation_limitation\": \"any\".\n- \"indicating_range\": 99999 m³.\n- \"resolution_of_indicating_device\": 0.0001 m³.\n- \"resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing\": omit (\"-\").\n- \"eut_testing_category\": \"Electromagnetic water meter\".\n- \"eut_testing_case\": \"Case D\" → value \"D\"? Keep \"Case D\" as string.\n- \"connection_type\": \"Flanged\".\n- \"minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe\": 0 mm.\n- \"minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe\": 0 mm.\n- \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": \"U0D0\".\n- \"flow_conditioner\": \"No\".\n- \"mounting\": omit.\n- \"orientation\": \"any\".\n- \"length\": 200 mm.\n- \"software_version\": \"V.1.04.0005.0004\".\n- \"crc_checksum\": \"9BA34F3B\".\n- \"cut_off\": 0.2 %.\n- \"detection_empty_pipe\": \"on\".\n- \"minimum_battery_life_time\": omit.\n- \"power_supply_type\", \"power_supply_voltage\", \"power_supply_frequency\", \"internal_backup_source_voltage\", \"internal_backup_battery_capacity\".\n\nNow assembling config_level with axis \"power_supply_module\":\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_module\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"V\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"HV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}},\n    {\"condition\": \"LV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 24, \"max\": 36}},\n    {\"condition\": \"LLV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 48}}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\npower_supply_type:\n- HV: \"AC\", LV: \"AC/DC\", LLV: \"DC\".\n\npower_supply_frequency:\n- HV {45,66}, LV {45,66}. LLV omitted.\n\ninternal_backup_source_voltage: HV 3.7 V, LV 3.7 V.\ninternal_backup_battery_capacity: HV 5200 mAh, LV 5200 mAh.\ninternal_backup